<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Sancocho! – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Sancocho!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify written instructions for peers. Concept: Relate concepts to everyday scenarios.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify food-related instructions for peers by pointing and using simple words (e.g., pointing to an apple and saying "Take an apple."). Can relate food vocabulary to everyday scenarios with gestures (e.g., points to lunch and says, "I eat chicken at lunch."). Can support understanding of food-related words by rephrasing instructions (e.g., says, "pick food" while holding an apple").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of various foods (apple, sancocho, rice with chicken, banana, sandwich). The teacher points to each and asks, "What's this food?" and "Do you like (apple)?" Students respond chorally or individually. The teacher asks, "What's your favorite food?" and encourages students to share, using "I like (sancocho)." Students practice pronunciation of /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack" with the teacher. The teacher uses gestures to show 'like' and 'don't like'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explains, "Today we will learn to explain food words and instructions to our friends in a simple way." The teacher holds up a picture of "chicken" and says "chicken." Then, the teacher holds up a picture of "rice with chicken" and points to the chicken, saying "chicken food." The teacher then holds up a flashcard with "Eat the banana" written on it. The teacher models simplifying: "Eat banana," while miming eating a banana. The teacher emphasizes using gestures and simple words to help someone understand.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use long words or short words to explain simply?" (Expected answer: "Short words!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use our hands to help explain?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat.")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple visual recipe card for a "Fruit Snack" with 3 steps, each with a picture and a short instruction. (e.g., Picture of an apple: "1. Take an apple." Picture of hands peeling: "2. Peel the banana." Picture of a bowl with fruit: "3. Eat fruit.")</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving information about preferences; sharing preferences.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the "Fruit Snack" recipe card. The teacher reads each step clearly, pointing to the words and pictures. Students repeat the instructions chorally.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then models simplifying each instruction for a peer, using gestures and simpler vocabulary:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"1. Take an apple." -> Teacher points to apple, says "Pick apple."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"2. Peel the banana." -> Teacher mimes peeling, says "Open banana."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"3. Eat fruit." -> Teacher points to fruit, mimes eating, says "Eat sweet."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher emphasizes pronunciation of blending /æ-p.əl/ in "apple" and /w-ɔːt-ər/ in "water" when relevant food items appear.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of a banana and asks, "What do we do to the banana?" Students respond, "Peel the banana!" or "Open banana!"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a picture of an apple and says, "Pick apple." Students point to the corresponding written instruction: "Take an apple."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying food-related instructions using simple words and gestures. Grammar: Present simple ("I like...", "I eat..."). Vocabulary: apple, banana, sandwich, snack, water, juice, eat, drink, tasty, sweet, healthy, hungry, favorite, prepare, serve.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Match & Simplify:</b> The teacher writes simple instructions on the board (e.g., "Drink water," "Prepare a sandwich"). Below, the teacher provides simplified options (e.g., "Water," "Make food"). Students, individually or chorally, match the instruction to its simpler version. For instance, for "Prepare a sandwich," students choose "Make food." The teacher explicitly models how "prepare" can be simplified to "make."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Rephrasing with Gestures:</b> The teacher gives students flashcards with pictures of actions related to food (e.g., a person eating soup, a person drinking juice, a person wanting a snack). The teacher says an instruction (e.g., "Please eat your soup."). Students, in pairs, rephrase the instruction using simpler words and gestures (e.g., "Eat soup!" while miming eating; "Drink juice!" while miming drinking). The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially /s/ in "soup" and "snack."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Explain My Food":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives 2-3 flashcards, each showing a different food item (e.g., sancocho, apple, juice). One student picks a card and says, "I like (sancocho), but I don't like (banana)." The partner's task is to *mediate* this by repeating or simplifying a key word from the sentence using gestures (e.g., for "I like sancocho," the partner points to the sancocho picture and says "Tasty soup!"). Students then switch roles.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat.")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative presentation and simplification of their "Food Recipe Book" pages.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Project 2: Food Recipe Book - Mediation Showcase:</b> Students bring their "Food Recipe Book" pages (from previous lessons), which include drawings of their favorite foods and simple sentences like "I like sancocho." or "I eat rice with chicken for lunch."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Student A shows their recipe book page to Student B. Student A reads their sentence (e.g., "I like sancocho.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B's task is to *mediate* the information to Student A, pretending Student A doesn't fully understand. Student B simplifies the message using gestures, pointing to the drawing, and simple words. For "I like sancocho," Student B might point to the sancocho and say, "Good soup! You enjoy!" or for "I eat rice with chicken for lunch," Student B points to rice and chicken, mimes eating, and says "Eat food at lunch."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to use modals like "can" (e.g., "You can eat this.") and WH- questions to check understanding (e.g., "What food is this?"). Students switch roles after 2-3 exchanges.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering assistance, ensuring students are using gestures and simple vocabulary, and providing feedback on effective simplification.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will select one sentence from their "Food Recipe Book" (or a given instruction like "Drink juice") and mediate it to their peer or the teacher, using simple words and at least one gesture.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of food words and target sounds (/f/, /s/, blending /apple/).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs words like 'apple', 'water', 'eat', 'drink', 'tasty'.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of simplified instruction:</b> The mediated message is easy for the peer to understand.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of gestures:</b> Effectively uses non-verbal cues to support understanding.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works respectfully with a peer, expressing likes/dislikes appropriately.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket by drawing a happy or sad face next to these questions:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I explain food words to my friend simply?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use my hands to help explain?"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about explaining food to your friend?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was hard about explaining food? What do you want to practice more?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The use of gestures and the "Food Recipe Book" as a tangible tool seemed effective for A1.1 students to practice simplifying. The distinction between "I like...but I don't..." was successfully integrated.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (new theme)? More focus on sentence frames for simplification or specific target vocabulary for rephrasing could be beneficial if students struggled with spontaneous simplification. Ensure sufficient visual aids for abstract concepts.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Like Sancocho!" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, building on the students' ability to understand and express basic information in English, incorporating new vocabulary and scenarios.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[2-3 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>